# 7. Database Design Principles

## 7.1 Naming convention

Use English table names in snake_case.

Examples:

```text
users
settings
account_types
accounts
category_groups
categories
payees
transactions
transfers
recurring_transactions
budgets
budget_categories
credit_cards
credit_card_invoices
credit_card_purchases
credit_card_installments
credit_card_invoice_payments
shopping_lists
shopping_list_items
purchases
purchase_items
```

## 7.2 Primary keys

All tables must use:

```text
id integer primary key
```

## 7.3 Timestamps

All tables must use:

```text
created_at
updated_at
```

## 7.4 Archival fields

Most user-created entities must include:

```text
archived boolean default false
archived_at timestamp nullable
```

Use archival for:

- Accounts
- Categories
- Payees
- Transactions
- Recurring transactions
- Budgets
- Credit cards
- Shopping lists
- Purchases

Do not archive purely relational join tables unless necessary.

## 7.5 Money fields

All money values must be stored as integer cents.

Example:

```text
amount_cents integer not null
```

Do not store money as floating point.

## 7.6 Dates

Use clear date fields:

```text
transaction_date
due_date
closing_date
payment_date
start_date
end_date
```

For SQLite, store dates as ISO strings using AdonisJS/Lucid conventions.

## 7.7 Currency

For the MVP, use a default currency from settings, probably:

```text
BRL
```

Future multi-currency support can be added later.

---

# 8. Database Schema Specification

---

## 8.1 Users

### Table: `users`

Purpose:

Stores application users.

Even if Fortuna starts as a personal app for one user, the database should support multiple users from the beginning.

Fields:

```text
id
full_name
email
password
created_at
updated_at
```

Suggested columns:

```ts
table.increments("id");
table.string("full_name").notNullable();
table.string("email").notNullable().unique();
table.string("password").notNullable();
table.timestamp("created_at");
table.timestamp("updated_at");
```

Relationships:

```text
User has one Settings
User has many Accounts
User has many Categories
User has many Transactions
User has many Budgets
User has many CreditCards
User has many ShoppingLists
```

Implementation notes:

- Password must be hashed.
- Email must be unique.
- Authentication should be implemented after the core project structure is stable.

---

## 8.2 Settings

### Table: `settings`

Purpose:

Stores user-specific configuration for locale, currency, and financial period rules.

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
locale
currency
fiscal_year_start_day
fiscal_year_start_month
created_at
updated_at
```

Suggested defaults:

```text
locale: pt-BR
currency: BRL
fiscal_year_start_day: 1
fiscal_year_start_month: 1
```

Important:

The fiscal year settings allow the user to define a custom financial year.

Example:

```text
fiscal_year_start_day = 25
fiscal_year_start_month = 12
```

This means the user can later generate financial reports using custom periods.

Relationships:

```text
Settings belongs to User
```

Validation rules:

- `locale` required
- `currency` required
- `fiscal_year_start_day` between 1 and 31
- `fiscal_year_start_month` between 1 and 12

---

## 8.3 Account Types

### Table: `account_types`

Purpose:

Defines types of financial accounts.

Examples:

```text
Checking account
Savings account
Cash wallet
Investment account
Digital wallet
Other
```

Fields:

```text
id
name
slug
description
created_at
updated_at
```

Suggested columns:

```ts
table.increments("id");
table.string("name").notNullable();
table.string("slug").notNullable().unique();
table.text("description").nullable();
table.timestamp("created_at");
table.timestamp("updated_at");
```

Relationships:

```text
AccountType has many Accounts
```

Seed data:

```text
checking
savings
cash
investment
digital_wallet
other
```

---

## 8.4 Accounts

### Table: `accounts`

Purpose:

Stores financial accounts owned by the user.

Examples:

```text
Main bank account
Cash wallet
Savings account
Investment account
Digital wallet
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
account_type_id
name
description
initial_balance_cents
current_balance_cents
currency
color
icon
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

Business rules:

- `initial_balance_cents` is the amount available when the account is created.
- `current_balance_cents` can be updated from transactions or calculated later.
- For the first version, it is acceptable to store `current_balance_cents`.
- Every transaction linked to an account affects its balance.
- Archived accounts should not appear in default lists.

Relationships:

```text
Account belongs to User
Account belongs to AccountType
Account has many Transactions
Account has many Transfers as source account
Account has many Transfers as destination account
Account has many CreditCards
```

---

## 8.5 Category Groups

### Table: `category_groups`

Purpose:

Groups categories into broader sections.

Examples:

```text
Income
Housing
Food
Transport
Health
Education
Leisure
Debt
Investments
Other
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
name
type
description
color
icon
sort_order
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

`type` should identify the general group direction:

```text
income
expense
transfer
```

Business rules:

- Category groups help organize reports.
- A category group can contain many categories.
- Category groups can be user-specific.
- Some default groups can be seeded for each user.

Relationships:

```text
CategoryGroup belongs to User
CategoryGroup has many Categories
```

---

## 8.6 Categories

### Table: `categories`

Purpose:

Classifies transactions.

Examples:

```text
Salary
Groceries
Rent
Fuel
Internet
School
Restaurant
Pharmacy
Credit card payment
Transfer
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
category_group_id
parent_id
name
type
description
color
icon
sort_order
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

`type` values:

```text
income
expense
transfer
```

Important:

A category can optionally have a parent category.

Example:

```text
Food
  Groceries
  Restaurant
  Coffee
```

Relationships:

```text
Category belongs to User
Category belongs to CategoryGroup
Category belongs to ParentCategory
Category has many ChildCategories
Category has many Transactions
Category has many BudgetCategories
```

Business rules:

- Income transactions should use income categories.
- Expense transactions should use expense categories.
- Transfers may use a special transfer category if needed.
- Archived categories should not appear in default selection fields.
- Existing transactions should keep their category even if the category is archived.

---

## 8.7 Payees

### Table: `payees`

Purpose:

Stores people, companies, stores, institutions, or entities involved in transactions.

Examples:

```text
Employer
Supermarket
Pharmacy
Electric company
Internet provider
School
Restaurant
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
name
document
email
phone
notes
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

Relationships:

```text
Payee belongs to User
Payee has many Transactions
Payee has many CreditCardPurchases
Payee has many Purchases
```

Business rules:

- Payee is optional in transactions.
- Payees improve filtering, reports, and transaction history.
- A payee should not be deleted if linked to existing records.

---

# 9. Core Financial Tables

---

## 9.1 Transactions

### Table: `transactions`

Purpose:

Stores financial movements in accounts.

Transaction types:

```text
income
expense
transfer
credit_card_payment
invoice_payment
adjustment
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
account_id
category_id
payee_id
type
description
notes
amount_cents
transaction_date
due_date
paid_at
status
payment_method
reference
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

`status` values:

```text
pending
paid
cancelled
scheduled
```

`payment_method` examples:

```text
cash
debit_card
credit_card
pix
bank_transfer
boleto
other
```

Business rules:

- Income increases account balance.
- Expense decreases account balance.
- Pending transactions should not affect current balance unless explicitly configured later.
- Paid transactions affect the balance.
- Transactions should support future filtering by:
  - Period
  - Account
  - Category
  - Payee
  - Type
  - Status

Relationships:

```text
Transaction belongs to User
Transaction belongs to Account
Transaction belongs to Category
Transaction belongs to Payee
Transaction may belong to Transfer
Transaction may belong to CreditCardInvoicePayment
Transaction may belong to Purchase
```

Important:

For MVP, transaction records are the central financial table.

---

## 9.2 Transfers

### Table: `transfers`

Purpose:

Represents money movement between two accounts owned by the same user.

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
source_account_id
destination_account_id
source_transaction_id
destination_transaction_id
amount_cents
transfer_date
description
notes
status
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

Business rules:

A transfer should generate two linked transactions:

1. Expense-like transaction from source account
2. Income-like transaction into destination account

Example:

```text
Transfer R$ 500 from Checking Account to Savings Account.
```

Effects:

```text
Checking Account: -50000 cents
Savings Account: +50000 cents
```

Relationships:

```text
Transfer belongs to User
Transfer belongs to SourceAccount
Transfer belongs to DestinationAccount
Transfer belongs to SourceTransaction
Transfer belongs to DestinationTransaction
```

Validation rules:

- Source account and destination account must be different.
- Amount must be greater than zero.
- Both accounts must belong to the same user.

---

## 9.3 Recurring Transactions

### Table: `recurring_transactions`

Purpose:

Stores repeated financial events.

Examples:

```text
Salary
Rent
Internet bill
Streaming subscription
School payment
Insurance
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
account_id
category_id
payee_id
type
description
amount_cents
frequency
interval
start_date
end_date
next_occurrence_date
last_generated_at
status
auto_create
notes
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

`frequency` values:

```text
daily
weekly
monthly
yearly
custom
```

`status` values:

```text
active
paused
finished
cancelled
```

Business rules:

- Recurring transactions should generate normal transactions.
- Generation can be manual in the first version.
- Automatic scheduling can be added later.
- `next_occurrence_date` must be updated after each generation.
- `auto_create` controls whether the system should create transactions automatically in future versions.

Relationships:

```text
RecurringTransaction belongs to User
RecurringTransaction belongs to Account
RecurringTransaction belongs to Category
RecurringTransaction belongs to Payee
RecurringTransaction has many Transactions in future version
```

MVP behavior:

- Show recurring transactions.
- Allow manual generation of the next transaction.
- Do not implement background jobs in the first step unless explicitly approved.

---

# 10. Budget Tables

---

## 10.1 Budgets

### Table: `budgets`

Purpose:

Stores planned financial limits or goals for a period.

Examples:

```text
Monthly grocery budget
Monthly transport budget
Family leisure budget
Annual education budget
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
name
description
period_type
start_date
end_date
amount_cents
alert_percentage
status
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

`period_type` values:

```text
monthly
yearly
custom
```

`status` values:

```text
active
closed
cancelled
```

Business rules:

- A budget can apply to one or more categories.
- Budget usage is calculated from transactions.
- Expenses should reduce available budget.
- Income budgets can be supported later, but MVP should focus on expense budgets.
- `alert_percentage` can be used later to warn the user when spending reaches a threshold.

Relationships:

```text
Budget belongs to User
Budget has many BudgetCategories
Budget belongs to many Categories through BudgetCategories
```

---

## 10.2 Budget Categories

### Table: `budget_categories`

Purpose:

Join table between budgets and categories.

Fields:

```text
id
budget_id
category_id
assigned_amount_cents
created_at
updated_at
```

Business rules:

- A budget may be split across multiple categories.
- `assigned_amount_cents` is optional if the budget has one global amount.
- If used, the sum of assigned amounts should not exceed the total budget amount.

Relationships:

```text
BudgetCategory belongs to Budget
BudgetCategory belongs to Category
```

---

# 11. Credit Card Tables

---

## 11.1 Credit Cards

### Table: `credit_cards`

Purpose:

Stores credit cards owned by the user.

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
account_id
name
brand
last_four_digits
credit_limit_cents
closing_day
due_day
color
icon
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

Examples of brand:

```text
visa
mastercard
elo
amex
hipercard
other
```

Business rules:

- A credit card belongs to a payment account.
- The account is used when paying the invoice.
- `closing_day` defines when the invoice closes.
- `due_day` defines when the invoice should be paid.
- Credit card purchases do not immediately affect account balance.
- Account balance is affected when the invoice is paid.

Relationships:

```text
CreditCard belongs to User
CreditCard belongs to Account
CreditCard has many CreditCardInvoices
CreditCard has many CreditCardPurchases
```

---

## 11.2 Credit Card Invoices

### Table: `credit_card_invoices`

Purpose:

Represents a monthly invoice for a credit card.

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
credit_card_id
reference_month
reference_year
opening_date
closing_date
due_date
total_amount_cents
paid_amount_cents
status
closed_at
paid_at
created_at
updated_at
```

`status` values:

```text
open
closed
partially_paid
paid
overdue
cancelled
```

Business rules:

- Purchases are attached to invoices.
- An invoice can be open, closed, partially paid, or paid.
- Closing an invoice should calculate its total.
- Paying an invoice should generate a transaction in the linked account.
- Invoice payment should reduce account balance.

Relationships:

```text
CreditCardInvoice belongs to User
CreditCardInvoice belongs to CreditCard
CreditCardInvoice has many CreditCardPurchases
CreditCardInvoice has many CreditCardInvoicePayments
```

---

## 11.3 Credit Card Purchases

### Table: `credit_card_purchases`

Purpose:

Stores purchases made with credit cards.

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
credit_card_id
credit_card_invoice_id
category_id
payee_id
description
notes
purchase_date
amount_cents
installments_count
status
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

`status` values:

```text
active
cancelled
refunded
```

Business rules:

- A single purchase may have one or many installments.
- For a one-time purchase, `installments_count = 1`.
- For an installment purchase, generate records in `credit_card_installments`.
- Purchase must be linked to a credit card.
- Purchase should be assigned to an invoice according to purchase date and card closing rules.

Relationships:

```text
CreditCardPurchase belongs to User
CreditCardPurchase belongs to CreditCard
CreditCardPurchase belongs to CreditCardInvoice
CreditCardPurchase belongs to Category
CreditCardPurchase belongs to Payee
CreditCardPurchase has many CreditCardInstallments
```

---

## 11.4 Credit Card Installments

### Table: `credit_card_installments`

Purpose:

Stores each installment of a credit card purchase.

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
credit_card_purchase_id
credit_card_invoice_id
installment_number
total_installments
amount_cents
due_date
status
created_at
updated_at
```

`status` values:

```text
pending
invoiced
paid
cancelled
```

Business rules:

- Installments are linked to invoices.
- Each installment belongs to one purchase.
- Each installment should have its own amount.
- The sum of installments should match the original purchase amount.
- If the amount cannot be divided equally, distribute the remaining cents in the first installment or last installment.

Relationships:

```text
CreditCardInstallment belongs to User
CreditCardInstallment belongs to CreditCardPurchase
CreditCardInstallment belongs to CreditCardInvoice
```

---

## 11.5 Credit Card Invoice Payments

### Table: `credit_card_invoice_payments`

Purpose:

Stores payments made toward credit card invoices.

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
credit_card_invoice_id
account_id
transaction_id
amount_cents
payment_date
notes
created_at
updated_at
```

Business rules:

- Paying an invoice creates a transaction.
- The transaction should be an expense or invoice payment type.
- Full payment sets invoice status to `paid`.
- Partial payment sets invoice status to `partially_paid`.
- Payment amount cannot exceed remaining invoice balance.

Relationships:

```text
CreditCardInvoicePayment belongs to User
CreditCardInvoicePayment belongs to CreditCardInvoice
CreditCardInvoicePayment belongs to Account
CreditCardInvoicePayment belongs to Transaction
```

---

# 12. Shopping Tables

---

## 12.1 Shopping Lists

### Table: `shopping_lists`

Purpose:

Stores reusable shopping lists.

Examples:

```text
Monthly groceries
Weekly groceries
Cleaning products
School supplies
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
name
description
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

Business rules:

- A shopping list can be reused.
- A shopping list does not directly affect finances.
- It can later be converted into one or more purchases.

Relationships:

```text
ShoppingList belongs to User
ShoppingList has many ShoppingListItems
```

---

## 12.2 Shopping List Items

### Table: `shopping_list_items`

Purpose:

Stores planned items inside a shopping list.

Fields:

```text
id
shopping_list_id
name
quantity
unit
estimated_price_cents
notes
checked
sort_order
created_at
updated_at
```

Business rules:

- Items can be checked or unchecked.
- Estimated price is optional.
- These items represent planning, not actual spending.

Relationships:

```text
ShoppingListItem belongs to ShoppingList
```

---

## 12.3 Purchases

### Table: `purchases`

Purpose:

Stores actual purchases made by the user.

Examples:

```text
Grocery purchase at supermarket
Pharmacy purchase
School supplies purchase
```

Fields:

```text
id
user_id
account_id
category_id
payee_id
shopping_list_id
transaction_id
description
purchase_date
total_amount_cents
payment_method
notes
archived
archived_at
created_at
updated_at
```

Business rules:

- A purchase can generate a transaction.
- A purchase can optionally originate from a shopping list.
- A purchase can have many purchase items.
- The purchase total should match the sum of purchase items, unless manually adjusted.

Relationships:

```text
Purchase belongs to User
Purchase belongs to Account
Purchase belongs to Category
Purchase belongs to Payee
Purchase belongs to ShoppingList
Purchase belongs to Transaction
Purchase has many PurchaseItems
```

---

## 12.4 Purchase Items

### Table: `purchase_items`

Purpose:

Stores actual purchased items.

Fields:

```text
id
purchase_id
name
quantity
unit
unit_price_cents
total_price_cents
notes
created_at
updated_at
```

Business rules:

- `total_price_cents` should usually equal `quantity * unit_price_cents`.
- Quantity can be decimal if needed, but MVP may store it as string or decimal-compatible value.
- Items are useful for analyzing grocery spending later.

Relationships:

```text
PurchaseItem belongs to Purchase
```

---

